cast:
  Grace:
  Ewa:
  Zofia:
  Kit:
  Doyle:
  Banda:
  Ada:
  Ruth:
~~~~

The withheld envelope
---------------------
Grace: Have we still got time to do this before Dad starts his shift? Because I have the envelope. I've had it since yesterday.
Zofia: The rota. You said you couldn't find it.
Grace: I could find it. I wanted you to stop looking for the Mace. I thought if you couldn't prove anything, perhaps you'd come home.
Zofia: With Mum still in Ruth's incident summary.
Grace: I wanted you home. I thought you’d be furious for a bit, and then—
Zofia: Mum would still be on that page.
Ewa: The envelope is shut. Keep it shut until I can check the copy against mine. Then my name stays on it.
Zofia: We could photograph it now.
Ewa: You could photograph the wrong page very efficiently. My signed copy first.
Grace: Dad can witness the handover before work. We said Albert Memorial, then Peter Pan. The meeting cards are in here separately. No writing on a monument, no leaving a packet under a bench.
Ewa: This handover is our own arrangement. Nobody at work ordered it. I am already out today; I will meet Samuel at Peter Pan. I am not going back across London for another unpaid evening.
Zofia: If Ruth asks you to explain—
Ewa: She can ask while I am paid. You have my evidence. You do not have my next shift.
Grace: Give me the map. I can get one thing to the right person at the right time.
Zofia: Give it to me. You can walk beside me.
Grace: All right. Is that the whole permission?
Zofia: For now.

What Kit saw
------------
Kit: Wait. Before we go. From the transfer. I need to tell it from the transfer, because last time I started with what I hadn't done and never got to what I saw.
Zofia: The case. Did you see it turn?
Kit: Yes. Tuesday, this week. Week four on our sheets. Fourteen forty. I saw Vale take the case away from the return route.
Doyle: My dear, the boy has named a man and a case. We have yet to hear what was inside. [class=dead]
Zofia: You recognised Vale. Did you see inside the case?
Kit: No. Same case shape, same man. I didn't see the Mace inside it. I need that written down too.
Grace: You said you hadn't seen anyone leave the route.
Kit: I lied. I thought if there hadn't been a diversion, there couldn't be a missing object. No. That's not what I thought. I thought they would stop the rehearsal, and then they would stop letting me help.
Zofia: Did anybody tell you to take a different route?
Kit: Vale said it had been cleared. I didn't ask by whom. I was holding a clipboard, which apparently makes me capable of recognising authority in other people with clipboards.
Grace: Can you say all that to somebody who can take a statement?
Kit: Can we sit down first?
Doyle: Fourteen forty is admirably precise, my dear. Was there a clock in sight? I should ask— [class=dead]
Zofia: My phone. It's the Workshop reply.
Grace: Can it wait?
Zofia: It's addressed to him. The Witness Workshop. That little publisher nobody had heard of. Their editor has put a signed, dated answer beside the old caption. Not another receipt.
Doyle: A signed answer at last! Read it, my dear; I have endured enough anonymous opinions. [class=dead]
Zofia: They've put your spiritualist writing back in. Your support for the Cottingley photographs too, and the evidence that they were fakes. The old caption stays beside the correction. People can check both.
Doyle: Well, my dear, they have allowed me my folly as well as my books. Now, that watch of his: we should ask whether— [class=dead]
Zofia: Whether what?
Kit: Zofia?
Zofia: He was asking about your clock.
Grace: Was the time on your clipboard, Kit, or did you look at a clock? I'll ask it.
Kit: I looked at my watch when I was late taking the clipboard back. Fourteen forty. I can write that. I can say what the watch was, too.
Zofia: He hasn't finished.
Grace: I know. You haven't told us the rest of his question. We can still ask ours.

Keep moving
-----------
Kit: The Round Pond. I think we want that way. A circle ought to be easy to find.
Grace: Look at the outline on the map. It's roughly a rectangle with rounded corners. Round Pond is its name. A circle isn't its shape.
Kit: Right. Sorry. Not you, Grace. The pond. I was looking at it when I apologised.
Grace: Did it accept?
Kit: It hasn't made its position clear.
Zofia: West of the Long Water. There. Peter Pan.
Kit: Can I stay here a minute? I can't go and tell your mum while you're looking at me like that.
Grace: If we miss Dad before his shift, who is going to witness it? Your mum isn't coming back for another trip, Zofia.
Zofia: Kit, I'm going. You can walk with us. You can tell me as we walk.
Kit: I thought you might say it would be all right.
Zofia: I don't know that. I can give you the blank side of a card.
Kit: Wait. First the date, then what I saw, then what I didn't see. Is that enough to start?
Zofia: Start with your name.
Kit: Kit Adeyemi. I saw Martin Vale divert the case on Tuesday in week four, at fourteen forty. I did not see inside it. I denied seeing the diversion afterwards.
Grace: I'll hold the map. Keep your eyes on the path when you're talking.
Kit: All this for somebody's private garden. William and Mary in 1689, then Caroline changing it. Did nobody ever leave a plan alone?
Zofia: Keep the water on our right. Peter Pan's on this side.
Kit: Nobody should need an envelope and three witnesses to find a statue.
Grace: You found it. It’s Mum you’re avoiding.
Kit: Your mum. Yes. Sorry, Zofia.

Names on the copy
-----------------
Ewa: The seal is intact. Grace, yours beside mine. Samuel, the heading first.
Banda: Week four. Tuesday. Six until ten. The hours match, and so does Ewa’s signature.
Ewa: I finished at ten. I left. I am not the afternoon shift on Ruth’s summary.
Zofia: Then she’s got the hours wrong. Cross them out—
Grace: Wait. Look at the other heading. Week two.
Ewa: My rehearsal shift, fourteen hundred to eighteen hundred. Keep Ruth’s summary too. That is the page that puts me at work after I went home.
Zofia: Mum, my empty-room note. Fourteen forty-five. Was that—
Ewa: The rehearsal. I cleaned beneath that trolley, then checked the empty room. Put the date on your note.
Zofia: I nearly did it too. Give me the pen.
Banda: I saw your mother leave after ten. At dispatch I saw Vale behind the visitor; Vale had the case. I saw him with it again at the receiving end.
Zofia: Open?
Banda: Closed on both occasions. I never saw inside.
Kit: Nor did I. But I saw him turn off the route at fourteen forty. This Tuesday. I’ve written the watch down. And the lie.
Ewa: Sign it, Kit.
Grace: Dad, why do we still do this? All these copies, then Zofia has to take them back to Ruth?
Banda: Because I saw that contractor and Ruth did not ask me. This time she gets my name beside his.
Grace: And if she wants another evening to discuss it?
Banda: Grace, loving the job does not mean owing it every evening. I have signed the receipt. We keep our originals; these copies go together.
Grace: I still have to explain the envelope.
Banda: Yes. You can do that without promising her tomorrow evening too.
Zofia: It still has to go to Ruth.
Ewa: With our names. She can ask us herself.

The unfinished condition
------------------------
Ada: Your letter gives a destination but no operation. What are you to do with the papers there? [class=dead]
Zofia: 'I request supervised access to compare the transfer documents.'
Ada: Comparison requires two sets of papers. Recovery requires the object. Which operation do you intend? [class=dead]
Zofia: I haven't asked to recover anything. I don't want to.
Grace: Are you changing it?
Zofia: Yes. She's helping me say exactly what I need. Ada. She's still here.
Ada: Then require the originals beside the copies. Without both, your proposed comparison cannot be performed. [class=dead]
Zofia: There's another reply. Separate message. Your caption.
Ada: The article and the Notes are separate quantities. Read whom they credit with each. [class=dead]
Zofia: The editor's signed it. They've checked the 1843 printing. Luigi Menabrea wrote the article. You translated it; Notes A to G are yours. They've fixed that and kept the old caption beside it.
Ada: Article, translation, Notes: three terms, correctly assigned. If another hand is to complete your comparison after— [class=dead]
Zofia: After what?
Grace: Zofia, your pen's on the paper.
Zofia: She was still working.
Grace: Do you want me to write it?
Zofia: No. Hold the corner. Please.
Grace: This corner?
Zofia: Yes. 'I request a supervised comparison of the enclosed copies with the originals. Ewa Kowalczyk and Samuel Banda must be named as the sources of their evidence. Kit Adeyemi asks to correct his earlier denial. I am not asking to enter the holding room or handle the missing object.'
Grace: You haven't promised they prove everything.
Zofia: They don't. I want her to receive them.
Grace: Sign it, then. I'll witness the delivery.

Receive it
----------
Grace: Vale. Across the path. He's seen the packet change hands.
Zofia: Keep the receipt flat. Don't fold it under the statement.
Ruth: Zofia, packet, keep it sealed. Tell me what you are submitting.
Zofia: Mum’s signed rota. Mr Banda’s account. Kit’s statement. Check them against the afternoon you put Mum in.
Ruth: Who is the person you are accusing?
Zofia: First receive the evidence.
Ruth: Name the person before I receive it.
